WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.150
As I film this, autumn has arrived in Arashiyama, Kyoto, and it's getting a little chilly,

2
00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:09.910
and I imagine some parts of Japan are already quite cold

3
00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.790
. The leaves in Arashiyama are starting to turn, and in about two

4
00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:19.710
weeks, the maple leaves should be bright red. In this class

5
00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.430
for Prosumer College, I want to talk carefully about fermenting winter vegetables

6
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.070
today I'd like to talk about napa cabbage properly.

7
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:34.210
Other winter vegetables too, like mizuna, which is delicious right now.

8
00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:39.030
For something a little unusual, what we often pickle at Kamoshika is

9
00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:43.870
this: king oyster mushrooms. You can pickle them too, and they're delicious.

10
00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:49.450
for chirimen sansho. It has a wonderful, wonderful fragrance, and

11
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:54.230
strong antibacterial and preservative effects, so we put sansho berries in our nukadoko

12
00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.630
getting more and more wrinkled. And it's a very cold time of year,

13
00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:04.550
so in that bitter cold, the daikon keeps losing water and getting smaller and smaller

14
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:06.750
as it dries.

15
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.430
So what does it look like when it's done? Like this.

16
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:17.890
come into season too, so I dry those and add the yuzu peel. And persimmons

17
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:22.830
are delicious too, so I dry the persimmon peel and add it. Apple peel too,

18
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:27.750
I keep adding all of these. They give it fragrance.

